From 14fcc8cbe7ff38c2461ecb858b8dc02443f9e767 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexanderNyr <alexander@auralite.local>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 09:01:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] X5 reference standards: ABI v2 (env2), ARC-20, ARC-721,
 ARC-M1 multisig
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three standards written in Rust against aura-sdk, and the ABI they need.

ABI v2 is a *second import module*, env2, not a new version of env: env is
frozen by 23 golden vectors and by every deployed contract, and a module
declares the surface it speaks by what it imports. Six functions —
calldata_len, calldata_read, return_write, caller, self_address,
abi_version — 1 KiB of calldata and 1 KiB of answer, both refused by the
session before the engine runs.

A caller-derived from and a self_address were forced by the standards: a
transfer(from, ...) with a caller-supplied from is permission to spend
anyone's tokens, and a multisig that cannot name itself cannot ask whether
it can cover a payment.

standards/arc20 12,733 B, standards/arc721 13,224 B, standards/multisig
15,268 B — 41,225 bytes, reproducible across two independent target
directories, pinned by size, sha256 and blake3, each with an abi.json.
43 new tests deploy the shipped bytecode through the real Lifecycle; 141
negative controls in total (6 new).

The rule the phase had to name: X2 commits any Returned(_), negative
included, so a standard's refusal is a status code and zero writes — every
gate asserts the empty change set, not a plausible balance.
